; AOT Test: a region whose page chain is longer than a recursive walk survives
; Expected: PASS
;
; Page.allocate used to reach the rest of a region's extension chain by
; recursing into current_page.allocate — one stack frame per chain link. The
; walk collapses the pages it visits, so it is almost always one step; what it
; never reaches is a page that filled up long ago and has not been an
; allocation START since. Its current_page still points at its immediate
; successor, and so does that one's, all the way to the tail.
;
; Such a page becomes a start whenever an allocation derives its page from an
; OBJECT rather than from the frame — String/Vector/Array all do, via
; Page.get(this) in their inits. So a handle created early in a long-lived
; region pays the whole chain on its first later allocation, and with recursion
; that is one frame per link: measured 2026-08-02, the wall sat at 104 485
; frames on the default 8 MB stack (600 002 at 64 MB — more stack only moved
; it). The largest region a program could hold was a function of its stack.
;
; Two things make this test actually reproduce that, and both are easy to lose:
;
;   1. The chain has to GROW UNCOLLAPSED. Every filler is hosted on the page of
;      the previous one, so each allocation walks exactly one step and leaves
;      page_k.current_page pointing at page_k+1. Growing it through one fixed
;      page instead lets the collapse fold the chain up behind you — that
;      version of this test built 153 000 pages and still had only 7 500 links.
;   2. The final allocation has to be TOO BIG for what the anchor left free on
;      its page. A small one fits in the remainder and never walks at all.
;
; The chain built here is 150 000 links (~600 MB), against a limit of 104 485.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.String

; Fill the region's first page so the anchor lands on a page of its own.
let pad String(3900 as size_t)

; The anchor's page is entered once and never allocated through again, so the
; chain from it is the uncollapsed one.
let anchor String(3900 as size_t)
let anchor_page Page.get(anchor.c_data())

; Seed the growth on the page after the anchor's, then extend link by link.
var prev String(3900 as size_t)
var i: size_t 0
while i < (150000 as size_t)
{
    let host Page.get(prev.c_data())
    let filler String^host(3900 as size_t)
    prev := filler
    i := i + (1 as size_t)
}

; One allocation hosted on the early page — the long walk.
let late String^anchor_page(3900 as size_t)

print "PASS"
